    "result_text": "All tests pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`). The suite consists of the `AcceptanceTest` class (both the pre-existing structure and the two new soft-delete checkpoints), and it's green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI changed owner deletion from a hard delete to a soft delete across the Spring layers:\n\n**Domain model** \u2014 `Owner.java`: added a `deleted` boolean field (`@Column(name = \"deleted\")`) with `isDeleted()`/`setDeleted()`. A newly created owner defaults to `false`.\n\n**Persistence schema** \u2014 added a `deleted BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE NOT NULL` column to the `owners` table in all four DB schemas (`h2`, `hsqldb`, `mysql`, `postgres`). I put `DEFAULT` before `NOT NULL` because HSQLDB (the test profile's DB) rejects the reverse order.\n\n**API contract** \u2014 `openapi.yml`: added a read-only `deleted` boolean property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and MapStruct auto-maps it from the entity. `GET /api/owners/{id}` now returns `deleted` (false for new, true after delete).\n\n**Service layer** \u2014 `ClinicServiceImpl.java`:\n- `deleteOwner(...)` now sets `deleted = true` and `save`s the owner instead of removing the row (returns 2xx, record retained). This is repository/profile-independent.\n- Added a `findActiveOwners()` helper and routed the create endpoint's **duplicate and identity checks** through it \u2014 `findHouseholdMembers` (the 409-blocking household duplicate), `countNamesakes`, and `deduplicateCustomerCode` \u2014 so owners flagged `deleted` are ignored. A normally-blocking duplicate is now allowed when the only matching owner has been soft-deleted.\n\nI intentionally left the daily-limit and city-capacity rules counting all rows, since those are capacity/rate limits rather than the duplicate/identity checks the spec scopes the change to.",
